00:22:19.200 --> 00:22:22.200
every model eval follows four steps

00:22:22.200 --> 00:22:29.200
first of all you decide what capabilities of LLM you want to test

00:22:29.200 --> 00:22:32.200
LLMs are general purpose models

00:22:32.200 --> 00:22:34.200
right, they can do a lot of things

00:22:34.200 --> 00:22:36.200
they have a lot of capabilities

00:22:36.200 --> 00:22:41.200
so there is no single model eval that can test every capability

00:22:41.200 --> 00:22:44.200
it's not like humans have IQ

00:22:44.200 --> 00:22:47.200
you can tell a lot about a human with a single number

00:22:47.200 --> 00:22:50.200
unfortunately LLMs don't have that

00:22:50.200 --> 00:22:54.200
every LLMs have a different model eval for every capability

00:22:54.200 --> 00:22:57.200
so the first thing that you have in model eval is

00:22:57.200 --> 00:23:02.200
that you decide what capability you want to test

00:23:02.200 --> 00:23:05.200
do you want to test its reasoning capability

00:23:05.200 --> 00:23:08.200
or its coding capability

00:23:08.200 --> 00:23:10.200
or do you want to see how safe it is to use

00:23:10.200 --> 00:23:14.200
or do you want to test its instruction following capability

00:23:14.200 --> 00:23:18.200
so the first thing that you do is you decide the capability that you want to test

00:23:18.200 --> 00:23:20.200
ok, after that what you do

00:23:20.200 --> 00:23:26.200
you bring a test to test that capability

00:23:26.200 --> 00:23:29.200
ok, you bring a test or a mechanism

00:23:29.200 --> 00:23:32.200
with which you will test that capability

00:23:32.200 --> 00:23:35.200
ok, I will talk about this a little later

00:23:35.200 --> 00:23:37.200
but the second step is that you bring a test

00:23:37.200 --> 00:23:43.200
third is that you run that model on that test

00:23:43.200 --> 00:23:46.200
basically that model gives the exam

00:23:46.200 --> 00:23:51.200
and during the exam you fix some things

00:23:51.200 --> 00:23:55.200
like which prompt you will send, what kind of conditions will be there

00:23:55.200 --> 00:23:59.200
you fix all these things so that you can repeat it further

00:23:59.200 --> 00:24:03.200
if you are testing multiple models then all the models should get one kind of condition

00:24:03.200 --> 00:24:07.200
so the third is you run the model under fixed protocol

00:24:07.200 --> 00:24:12.200
and the fourth thing is once the test is done you score and interpret

00:24:12.200 --> 00:24:13.200
it is simple

00:24:13.200 --> 00:24:18.200
so if we talk about the four steps then you first decide which capability to test

00:24:18.200 --> 00:24:24.200
to test that capability you bring a test for the exam

00:24:24.200 --> 00:24:28.200
then you run that test in a controlled environment

00:24:28.200 --> 00:24:32.200
and you publish or interpret the score

00:24:32.200 --> 00:24:34.200
so these are the four steps

00:24:34.200 --> 00:24:37.200
let's talk about the second important step

00:24:37.200 --> 00:24:42.200
where we said that we bring a test

00:24:42.200 --> 00:24:45.200
there are two types of tests in model evals

00:24:45.200 --> 00:24:47.200
see here I have written it down

00:24:47.200 --> 00:24:50.200
the first test is called benchmark

00:24:50.200 --> 00:24:52.200
maybe you have heard its name

00:24:52.200 --> 00:24:54.200
there are very famous benchmarks

00:24:54.200 --> 00:24:56.200
what are the benchmarks?

00:24:56.200 --> 00:25:00.200
benchmarks are basically standardized shared tests

00:25:00.200 --> 00:25:04.200
so if there is a test like MMLU or SWI bench

00:25:04.200 --> 00:25:06.200
because everyone runs the same test

00:25:06.200 --> 00:25:09.200
it is great for comparing models on common ground

00:25:09.200 --> 00:25:12.200
so benchmark is a type of test

00:25:12.200 --> 00:25:14.200
benchmark is like a standardized test

00:25:14.200 --> 00:25:18.200
it is basically the same for all the models

00:25:18.200 --> 00:25:23.200
and you can openly compare the numbers of the two models

00:25:23.200 --> 00:25:25.200
it is like a standardized thing

00:25:25.200 --> 00:25:28.200
everyone recognizes it and everyone uses it

00:25:28.200 --> 00:25:30.200
and the second thing is that

00:25:30.200 --> 00:25:35.200
rather than testing an LLM on the benchmark

00:25:35.200 --> 00:25:39.200
you make your own evaluation sets

00:25:39.200 --> 00:25:41.200
see here it is written

00:25:49.200 --> 00:25:51.200
this is a very important point

00:25:51.200 --> 00:25:52.200
understand this

00:25:52.200 --> 00:25:54.200
I also said an important point

00:25:54.200 --> 00:25:57.200
that model evals are of two types

00:25:58.200 --> 00:26:00.200
one is benchmarks

00:26:01.200 --> 00:26:04.200
which are basically standardized tests

00:26:04.200 --> 00:26:06.200
which test specific capabilities

00:26:06.200 --> 00:26:08.200
like the capability of maths

00:26:08.200 --> 00:26:11.200
or the capability of reasoning

00:26:11.200 --> 00:26:14.200
