01:00:39.200 --> 01:00:41.200
the evaluations of two models

01:00:41.200 --> 01:00:43.200
then while running both the models

01:00:43.200 --> 01:00:45.200
all the settings should be

01:00:45.200 --> 01:00:47.200
the same

01:00:47.200 --> 01:00:49.200
so the settings are told in the run configuration

01:00:49.200 --> 01:00:51.200
so there are

01:00:51.200 --> 01:00:53.200
three parts

01:00:53.200 --> 01:00:55.200
first is prompt

01:00:55.200 --> 01:00:57.200
second is decoding

01:00:57.200 --> 01:00:59.200
and sampling configuration

01:00:59.200 --> 01:01:01.200
and third is your scoring strategy

01:01:01.200 --> 01:01:03.200
and environment

01:01:03.200 --> 01:01:05.200
I will tell you about all three

01:01:05.200 --> 01:01:07.200
so in prompt construction

01:01:07.200 --> 01:01:09.200
you are told that

01:01:09.200 --> 01:01:11.200
when you write the prompt

01:01:11.200 --> 01:01:13.200
and send it

01:01:13.200 --> 01:01:15.200
from here

01:01:15.200 --> 01:01:17.200
you will pick a question

01:01:17.200 --> 01:01:19.200
and send it to the model

01:01:19.200 --> 01:01:21.200
the model will give the answer

01:01:21.200 --> 01:01:23.200
but along with sending this question

01:01:23.200 --> 01:01:25.200
you also send a system prompt

01:01:25.200 --> 01:01:27.200
so how you write

01:01:27.200 --> 01:01:29.200
the system prompt

01:01:29.200 --> 01:01:31.200
that also matters

01:01:31.200 --> 01:01:33.200
if the prompt is different then one model will give different result

01:01:33.200 --> 01:01:35.200
and the other will give different result

01:01:35.200 --> 01:01:37.200
I guess you understood

01:01:37.200 --> 01:01:39.200
so in prompt construction

01:01:39.200 --> 01:01:41.200
you define that your system prompt

01:01:41.200 --> 01:01:43.200
will be zero shot

01:01:43.200 --> 01:01:45.200
or few shot

01:01:45.200 --> 01:01:47.200
it's simple meaning is

01:01:47.200 --> 01:01:49.200
when you are sending this

01:01:49.200 --> 01:01:51.200
question to your LLM

01:01:51.200 --> 01:01:53.200
by taking out the answer

01:01:53.200 --> 01:01:55.200
then along with that

01:01:55.200 --> 01:01:57.200
you want to show some solved examples

01:01:57.200 --> 01:01:59.200
and send it

01:01:59.200 --> 01:02:01.200
or you want to give it without any example

01:02:01.200 --> 01:02:03.200
so this is called

01:02:03.200 --> 01:02:05.200
zero shot

01:02:05.200 --> 01:02:07.200
zero shot means you simply

01:02:07.200 --> 01:02:09.200
picked up the question and gave it

01:02:09.200 --> 01:02:11.200
and

01:02:11.200 --> 01:02:13.200
few shot means

01:02:13.200 --> 01:02:15.200
you solved some questions like this

01:02:15.200 --> 01:02:17.200
in the prompt

01:02:17.200 --> 01:02:19.200
and then at last you sent a question

01:02:19.200 --> 01:02:21.200
that you solved these questions like this

01:02:21.200 --> 01:02:23.200
and now solve this question

01:02:23.200 --> 01:02:25.200
and tell me

01:02:25.200 --> 01:02:27.200
so in prompt construction

01:02:27.200 --> 01:02:29.200
in benchmark you are told

01:02:29.200 --> 01:02:31.200
that you want to keep zero shot

01:02:31.200 --> 01:02:33.200
or few shot

01:02:33.200 --> 01:02:35.200
now if you talk about GSM 8k

01:02:35.200 --> 01:02:37.200
GSM 8k

01:02:37.200 --> 01:02:39.200
uses few shot strategy

01:02:39.200 --> 01:02:41.200
as written here

01:02:41.200 --> 01:02:43.200
that GSM 8k

01:02:43.200 --> 01:02:45.200
is classically reported

01:02:45.200 --> 01:02:47.200
eight shot, meaning whenever you

01:02:47.200 --> 01:02:49.200
send a question to LLM

01:02:49.200 --> 01:02:51.200
then you show it first

01:02:51.200 --> 01:02:53.200
eight solved examples

01:02:53.200 --> 01:02:55.200
in system prompt

01:02:55.200 --> 01:02:57.200
and then you send your question

01:02:57.200 --> 01:02:59.200
obviously if you keep zero shot

01:02:59.200 --> 01:03:01.200
then the performance of model will be bad

01:03:01.200 --> 01:03:03.200
and if you keep few shots

01:03:03.200 --> 01:03:05.200
then your performance will improve

01:03:05.200 --> 01:03:07.200
so in GSM 8k

01:03:07.200 --> 01:03:09.200
few shot is kept

01:03:09.200 --> 01:03:11.200
second, are you allowing

01:03:11.200 --> 01:03:13.200
chain of thought

01:03:13.200 --> 01:03:15.200
or are you telling it to work directly

01:03:15.200 --> 01:03:17.200
chain of thought, if you are allowing

01:03:17.200 --> 01:03:19.200
then you are sending some kind of system prompt

01:03:19.200 --> 01:03:21.200
that see this question

01:03:21.200 --> 01:03:23.200
and try to solve it

01:03:23.200 --> 01:03:25.200
step by step

01:03:25.200 --> 01:03:27.200
like here the answer is also shown

01:03:27.200 --> 01:03:29.200
Natalia sold 48

01:03:29.200 --> 01:03:31.200
divided by 2

01:03:31.200 --> 01:03:33.200
means 24 clips in may

01:03:33.200 --> 01:03:35.200
Natalia sold 48 plus 24

01:03:35.200 --> 01:03:37.200
clips all together

01:03:37.200 --> 01:03:39.200
this is getting solved step by step and is reaching the answer

01:03:39.200 --> 01:03:41.200
if you are not allowing chain of thought

01:03:41.200 --> 01:03:43.200
and you are telling

01:03:43.200 --> 01:03:45.200
to directly answer this

01:03:45.200 --> 01:03:47.200
then chances of mistakes are more

01:03:47.200 --> 01:03:49.200
so in GSM 8k also

01:03:49.200 --> 01:03:51.200
chain of thought is allowed

01:03:51.200 --> 01:03:53.200
again, here we are just talking

01:03:53.200 --> 01:03:55.200
about the options available

01:03:55.200 --> 01:03:57.200
while constructing the prompt

01:03:57.200 --> 01:03:59.200
few shot, zero shot, chain of thought

01:03:59.200 --> 01:04:01.200
or normal

01:04:01.200 --> 01:04:03.200
after that

01:04:03.200 --> 01:04:05.200
you see things

01:04:05.200 --> 01:04:07.200
in your decoding and sampling configuration

01:04:07.200 --> 01:04:09.200
you keep temperature value

01:04:09.200 --> 01:04:11.200
around zero

01:04:11.200 --> 01:04:13.200
because if you increase it more than zero

01:04:13.200 --> 01:04:15.200
then creative answers start coming

01:04:15.200 --> 01:04:17.200
and every time they start varying

01:04:17.200 --> 01:04:19.200
so temperature is mostly around zero

01:04:19.200 --> 01:04:21.200
for such benchmarks

01:04:21.200 --> 01:04:23.200
then you set a max token

01:04:23.200 --> 01:04:25.200
if you set a very less max token

01:04:25.200 --> 01:04:27.200
then it can be

01:04:27.200 --> 01:04:29.200
since chain of thought is triggered

01:04:29.200 --> 01:04:31.200
and while thinking in the reach

01:04:31.200 --> 01:04:33.200
the answer is over and model

01:04:33.200 --> 01:04:35.200
was not able to generate the answer

01:04:35.200 --> 01:04:37.200
versus if you have allowed too much tokens

01:04:37.200 --> 01:04:39.200
then what can a very powerful model do

01:04:39.200 --> 01:04:41.200
it can reason very strongly

01:04:41.200 --> 01:04:43.200
so you specify max token

01:04:43.200 --> 01:04:45.200
that I will give max token to model A

01:04:45.200 --> 01:04:47.200
and max token to model B

01:04:47.200 --> 01:04:49.200
it is not very less or very much

01:04:49.200 --> 01:04:51.200
so this is the second setting

01:04:51.200 --> 01:04:53.200
after that

01:04:53.200 --> 01:04:55.200
this is a very interesting thing

01:04:55.200 --> 01:04:57.200
that you

01:04:57.200 --> 01:04:59.200
finalize the scoring strategy

01:04:59.200 --> 01:05:01.200
what is scoring strategy

01:05:01.200 --> 01:05:03.200
one scoring strategy is

01:05:03.200 --> 01:05:05.200
pass at the rate 1

01:05:05.200 --> 01:05:07.200
this means that you

01:05:07.200 --> 01:05:09.200
show your model

01:05:09.200 --> 01:05:11.200
one question

01:05:11.200 --> 01:05:13.200
once, it gave the answer

01:05:13.200 --> 01:05:15.200
now that answer will be either right or wrong

01:05:15.200 --> 01:05:17.200
so this is called

01:05:17.200 --> 01:05:19.200
pass at the rate 1

01:05:19.200 --> 01:05:21.200
then comes your

01:05:21.200 --> 01:05:23.200
pass at the rate k

01:05:23.200 --> 01:05:25.200
what happens in this is that if you

01:05:25.200 --> 01:05:27.200
did pass at the rate 5

01:05:27.200 --> 01:05:29.200
then you will ask

01:05:29.200 --> 01:05:31.200
one question 5 times

01:05:31.200 --> 01:05:33.200
from the same model

01:05:33.200 --> 01:05:35.200
and if he answered

01:05:35.200 --> 01:05:37.200
at least once out of 5 times

01:05:37.200 --> 01:05:39.200
then you will assume that model gave the right answer

01:05:39.200 --> 01:05:41.200
so basically pass at the k is

01:05:41.200 --> 01:05:43.200
a more lenient strategy

01:05:43.200 --> 01:05:45.200
to evaluate your LLM

01:05:45.200 --> 01:05:47.200
pass at the rate 1 is a more

01:05:47.200 --> 01:05:49.200
strict strategy

01:05:49.200 --> 01:05:51.200
so again you have to decide that you have to keep

01:05:51.200 --> 01:05:53.200
pass at the rate 1 and pass at the rate k

01:05:53.200 --> 01:05:55.200
will be the same during the evaluation

01:05:55.200 --> 01:05:57.200
so in GSM 8k

01:05:57.200 --> 01:05:59.200
actually, I don't remember

01:05:59.200 --> 01:06:01.200
I have to see the paper

01:06:01.200 --> 01:06:03.200
most likely pass at the rate 1 is used

01:06:03.200 --> 01:06:05.200
but in some benchmarks

01:06:05.200 --> 01:06:07.200
pass at the rate k can also be used

01:06:07.200 --> 01:06:09.200
depending on how difficult

01:06:09.200 --> 01:06:11.200
the question is

01:06:11.200 --> 01:06:13.200
then you have one more

01:06:13.200 --> 01:06:15.200
majority at the rate 8

01:06:15.200 --> 01:06:17.200
majority at the rate k is basically

01:06:17.200 --> 01:06:19.200
what happens in this is that

01:06:19.200 --> 01:06:21.200
you ask the same question

01:06:21.200 --> 01:06:23.200
from your model

01:06:23.200 --> 01:06:25.200
it gives you

01:06:25.200 --> 01:06:27.200
k different answers

01:06:27.200 --> 01:06:29.200
the answer that comes most often

01:06:29.200 --> 01:06:31.200
you assume

01:06:31.200 --> 01:06:33.200
that answer

01:06:33.200 --> 01:06:35.200
basically mode

01:06:35.200 --> 01:06:37.200
the mode is mean-median mode

01:06:37.200 --> 01:06:39.200
so you assume the mode as the answer

01:06:39.200 --> 01:06:41.200
so whenever you

01:06:41.200 --> 01:06:43.200
read in a benchmark

01:06:43.200 --> 01:06:45.200
someone said that

01:06:45.200 --> 01:06:47.200
in a particular benchmark

01:06:47.200 --> 01:06:49.200
82% is scored by our model

01:06:49.200 --> 01:06:51.200
so you should ask

01:06:51.200 --> 01:06:53.200
that pass at the rate

01:06:53.200 --> 01:06:55.200
1 strategy is used

01:06:55.200 --> 01:06:57.200
pass at the rate k strategy is used

01:06:57.200 --> 01:06:59.200
or majority at the rate k strategy is used

01:06:59.200 --> 01:07:01.200
different scoring strategies

01:07:01.200 --> 01:07:03.200
are used so you should know that

01:07:03.200 --> 01:07:05.200
I hope you are understanding

01:07:05.200 --> 01:07:07.200
then

01:07:07.200 --> 01:07:09.200
do you want to allow

01:07:09.200 --> 01:07:11.200
tools while evaluating the benchmark

01:07:11.200 --> 01:07:13.200
or not

01:07:13.200 --> 01:07:15.200
this is also told from the beginning

01:07:15.200 --> 01:07:17.200
because if you allow tools

01:07:17.200 --> 01:07:19.200
for example one tool is web search

01:07:19.200 --> 01:07:21.200
if you allow web search

