and they help us in knowing

01:44:37.480 --> 01:44:39.480
about any particular LLM capability

01:44:39.480 --> 01:44:41.480
but it's not like

01:44:41.480 --> 01:44:43.480
benchmarks are flawless

01:44:43.480 --> 01:44:45.480
perfect

01:44:45.480 --> 01:44:47.480
and you always believe in

01:44:47.480 --> 01:44:49.480
the information you get from it

01:44:49.480 --> 01:44:51.480
in many scenarios

01:44:51.480 --> 01:44:53.480
benchmarks are misleading

01:44:53.480 --> 01:44:55.480
so you can't blindly trust them

01:44:55.480 --> 01:44:57.480
you have to be

01:44:57.480 --> 01:44:59.480
very very careful

01:44:59.480 --> 01:45:01.480
if you are reading benchmarks

01:45:01.480 --> 01:45:03.480
you have to be very very careful

01:45:03.480 --> 01:45:05.480
there are 3-4 types of problems in benchmarks

01:45:05.480 --> 01:45:07.480
let's discuss them one by one

01:45:07.480 --> 01:45:09.480
the first problem is benchmark contamination

01:45:09.480 --> 01:45:11.480
what does it mean

01:45:11.480 --> 01:45:13.480
it means very simple

01:45:13.480 --> 01:45:15.480
many of your benchmarks

01:45:15.480 --> 01:45:17.480
MMLU, GSM 8

01:45:17.480 --> 01:45:19.480
and many more

01:45:19.480 --> 01:45:21.480
they are mostly

01:45:21.480 --> 01:45:23.480
public benchmarks

01:45:23.480 --> 01:45:25.480
what does it mean to have a public benchmark

01:45:25.480 --> 01:45:27.480
that their research paper

01:45:27.480 --> 01:45:29.480
their methodology

01:45:29.480 --> 01:45:31.480
and their data set

01:45:31.480 --> 01:45:33.480
are all publicly available on the internet

01:45:35.480 --> 01:45:37.480
and specially those benchmarks

01:45:37.480 --> 01:45:39.480
that came very early in 2021

01:45:39.480 --> 01:45:41.480
2022, 2023

01:45:41.480 --> 01:45:43.480
their data set

01:45:43.480 --> 01:45:45.480
is available on the internet

01:45:45.480 --> 01:45:47.480
for a long time

01:45:47.480 --> 01:45:49.480
now imagine that recently 6 months ago

01:45:49.480 --> 01:45:51.480
OpenAI

01:45:51.480 --> 01:45:53.480
trained 5.6

01:45:53.480 --> 01:45:55.480
so

01:45:55.480 --> 01:45:57.480
in this

01:45:57.480 --> 01:45:59.480
they scraped the most recent

01:45:59.480 --> 01:46:01.480
internet data

01:46:01.480 --> 01:46:03.480
so there is a good chance

01:46:03.480 --> 01:46:05.480
that when you are

01:46:05.480 --> 01:46:07.480
scrapping the internet data

01:46:07.480 --> 01:46:09.480
and putting it in pre-training

01:46:09.480 --> 01:46:11.480
then the data set

01:46:11.480 --> 01:46:13.480
of this public benchmark

01:46:13.480 --> 01:46:15.480
data set means questions and their correct answers

01:46:15.480 --> 01:46:17.480
also become

01:46:17.480 --> 01:46:19.480
a part of this model's pre-training

01:46:19.480 --> 01:46:21.480
now if he has seen

01:46:21.480 --> 01:46:23.480
all 8000-8500 questions and answers

01:46:23.480 --> 01:46:25.480
then in future

01:46:25.480 --> 01:46:27.480
when you ask him

01:46:27.480 --> 01:46:29.480
to tell you the answer to this question

01:46:29.480 --> 01:46:31.480
there is no guarantee that he is thinking

01:46:31.480 --> 01:46:33.480
or he has memorized

01:46:33.480 --> 01:46:35.480
so that is why

01:46:35.480 --> 01:46:37.480
for a long time

01:46:37.480 --> 01:46:39.480
your big models

01:46:39.480 --> 01:46:41.480
who have done pre-training on a big data set

01:46:41.480 --> 01:46:43.480
for them these benchmarks are

01:46:43.480 --> 01:46:45.480
contaminated because they already know the answer

01:46:45.480 --> 01:46:47.480
because they became a part of their training data

01:46:47.480 --> 01:46:49.480
so this is a big problem

01:46:49.480 --> 01:46:51.480
specially with public

01:46:51.480 --> 01:46:53.480
benchmarks so the solution

01:46:53.480 --> 01:46:55.480
is to use private

01:46:55.480 --> 01:46:57.480
benchmarks

01:46:57.480 --> 01:46:59.480
there are many private benchmarks

01:46:59.480 --> 01:47:01.480
we will discuss in the next class

01:47:01.480 --> 01:47:03.480
or use dynamic benchmarks

01:47:03.480 --> 01:47:05.480
these are like static benchmarks

01:47:05.480 --> 01:47:07.480
once the data set

01:47:07.480 --> 01:47:09.480
published in research

01:47:09.480 --> 01:47:11.480
is the same data set since last 5 years

01:47:11.480 --> 01:47:13.480
but there are certain dynamic benchmarks as well

01:47:13.480 --> 01:47:15.480
where on a daily basis

01:47:15.480 --> 01:47:17.480
or in a particular window

01:47:17.480 --> 01:47:19.480
the data set keeps updating

01:47:19.480 --> 01:47:21.480
so those types of data sets

01:47:21.480 --> 01:47:23.480
can be used

01:47:23.480 --> 01:47:25.480
but benchmark contamination is a proper thing

01:47:25.480 --> 01:47:27.480
second

01:47:27.480 --> 01:47:29.480
there is one more thing

01:47:29.480 --> 01:47:31.480
benchmark saturation

01:47:31.480 --> 01:47:33.480
benchmark saturation is a process

01:47:33.480 --> 01:47:35.480
where a benchmark

01:47:35.480 --> 01:47:37.480
comes in the picture

01:47:37.480 --> 01:47:39.480
let's say in 2021

01:47:39.480 --> 01:47:41.480
so now this is new

01:47:41.480 --> 01:47:43.480
and no model has

01:47:43.480 --> 01:47:45.480
any idea about this benchmark

01:47:45.480 --> 01:47:47.480
no one has seen it

01:47:47.480 --> 01:47:49.480
so on these questions

01:47:49.480 --> 01:47:51.480
most of the times your models

01:47:51.480 --> 01:47:53.480
poorly perform

01:47:53.480 --> 01:47:55.480
25% score or 36% score

01:47:55.480 --> 01:47:57.480
all the models are doing this

01:47:57.480 --> 01:47:59.480
but over time

01:47:59.480 --> 01:48:01.480
the benchmark is static

01:48:01.480 --> 01:48:03.480
there are no changes in this

01:48:03.480 --> 01:48:05.480
but your models are improving

01:48:05.480 --> 01:48:07.480
every 6 months

01:48:07.480 --> 01:48:09.480
so their performance

01:48:09.480 --> 01:48:11.480
over these questions improve over time

01:48:11.480 --> 01:48:13.480
so you will see that

01:48:13.480 --> 01:48:15.480
new model starts performing better

01:48:15.480 --> 01:48:17.480
last generation of models

01:48:17.480 --> 01:48:19.480
were at 36%

01:48:19.480 --> 01:48:21.480
now they are at 50%

01:48:21.480 --> 01:48:23.480
and then 70%

01:48:23.480 --> 01:48:25.480
over time your models

01:48:25.480 --> 01:48:27.480
start doing over 90-95%

01:48:27.480 --> 01:48:29.480
and all the models cluster at the same place

01:48:29.480 --> 01:48:31.480
so there is no difference in score

01:48:31.480 --> 01:48:33.480
between them

01:48:33.480 --> 01:48:35.480
opus 9.5

01:48:35.480 --> 01:48:37.480
your most recent model

01:48:37.480 --> 01:48:39.480
is at 95%

